# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-first-way.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aquinas-first-way.logic.r1 has "□(in_potency ∧ mover_in_act ∧ self_mover → act_pot_same)" and "motion_is_reduction ∧ reduction_needs_actual ∧ ¬self_mover → moved_by_another" and "some_motion ∧ moved_by_another → other_movers" and "some_motion ∧ moved_by_another ∧ ¬infinite_regress → first_unmoved"; aquinas-first-way.logic.r1.2 has "in_potency ∧ mover_in_act → □(self_mover → act_pot_same)" and "some_motion ∧ ¬self_mover → moved_by_another" and "some_motion ∧ moved_by_another → other_movers" and "¬infinite_regress → first_unmoved"; they differ when only "in the world some things are in motion", "whatever is in motion is in potentiality to that towards which it is in motion", "a thing moves inasmuch as it is in act", "motion is nothing else than the reduction of something from potentiality to actuality", "nothing can be reduced from potentiality to actuality except by something in a state of actuality", "a thing is both mover and moved in the same respect and way, i.e. moves itself", "whatever is in motion is put in motion by another", "the series of movers goes on to infinity", "there is any other mover", "we arrive at a first mover, put in motion by no other" and "necessarily, (if (whatever is in motion is in potentiality to that towards which it is in motion and a thing moves inasmuch as it is in act and a thing is both mover and moved in the same respect and way, i.e. moves itself), then the same thing is at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect)" are true, which the first allows; aquinas-first-way.logic.r1.3 has "□(self_mover → act_pot_same)" and "in_potency ∧ mover_in_act ∧ motion_is_reduction ∧ reduction_needs_actual ∧ ¬self_mover → moved_by_another" and "some_motion ∧ moved_by_another → other_movers" and "some_motion ∧ moved_by_another ∧ ¬infinite_regress → first_unmoved"
aquinas-first-way.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: aquinas-first-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r1.3 take for granted "if (whatever is in motion is in potentiality to that towards which it is in motion and a thing moves inasmuch as it is in act), then (necessarily, (if a thing is both mover and moved in the same respect and way, i.e. moves itself, then the same thing is at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect))"; aquinas-first-way.logic.r1 does not
aquinas-first-way.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-first-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (whatever is in motion is in potentiality to that towards which it is in motion and a thing moves inasmuch as it is in act), then (necessarily, (if a thing is both mover and moved in the same respect and way, i.e. moves itself, then the same thing is at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect))"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
aquinas-first-way.logic.r1: argue/readings: U2: aquinas-first-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r1.3 take for granted "necessarily, (if a thing is both mover and moved in the same respect and way, i.e. moves itself, then the same thing is at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect)"; aquinas-first-way.logic.r1 does not
aquinas-first-way.logic.r1: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-first-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "necessarily, (if a thing is both mover and moved in the same respect and way, i.e. moves itself, then the same thing is at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
